### Animating the sound

You can animate a clip's sound with actions. While an action is selected, the Volume row, Mute and the fade lengths write that action's end state, and so do Pan, Mix to mono, Channels, the EQ, Hum, Voice Isolation's Amount, Noise Reduction, the Gate, the Leveller, the De-esser, the Compressor and the Limiter. Numbers move smoothly across the action's span: a pan sweeps from one side to the other, an EQ band's gain rises, a compressor's threshold drifts, the isolated voice fades in. Switches change at the action's end, with no glide: Mix to mono, Channels, EQ Bypass, a pass filter's slope, Hum's mains and harmonics, the Leveller's Response, and turning a section or an EQ slot on or off. Unticking a section or an EQ slot with an action selected turns it off at the action's end. What you hear in the preview is what an export writes.

A clip whose own Voice Isolation is off still gets its isolated voice made when an action turns the Amount up, and the gate, the leveller and the de-esser measure the clip's loudness when an action turns them on. A limiter or noise reduction turned on by an action runs for the clip's whole track from the start, as one set on the clip itself does.

Ducking, Keep pitch, the fade shapes and the voice effect can't be animated, and neither can a nest clip's Mix to mono. They always change the clip's own settings, even with an action selected.

## EQ

The EQ shapes the clip's tone. Its first row draws the response: how much each frequency from 20 Hz to 20 kHz is turned up or down, the hum notches included. The line is greyed while Bypass is on, and is not drawn when the selected clips' settings differ.

| Control | What it does | Range | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bypass | Silences the EQ without losing its settings, to compare with and without. | on / off | off |
| Rumble Cut | Turns the High-pass on at 80 Hz and 24 dB/oct: takes out traffic, handling noise and desk thumps. | — | — |
| De-mud | Sets Band 1 to −3 dB at 300 Hz: less boom and boxiness. | — | — |
| Presence | Sets Band 3 to +3 dB at 4 kHz: words easier to follow. | — | — |
| High-pass | Turns down everything below its Frequency. Slope 12 or 24 dB/oct. | 20–20000 Hz | 80 Hz, 12 dB/oct |
| Low shelf | Turns everything below its Frequency up or down by its Gain. | ±24 dB | 100 Hz, 0 dB |
| Band 1, 2, 3 | Turns the frequencies around its Frequency up or down by its Gain; Q sets how narrow (higher is narrower). Quick picks for Q: 0.5, 0.7, 1, 2, 4. | ±24 dB, Q 0.1–10 | 300, 1000, 4000 Hz; 0 dB; Q 1 |
| High shelf | Turns everything above its Frequency up or down by its Gain. | ±24 dB | 8000 Hz, 0 dB |
| Low-pass | Turns down everything above its Frequency. Slope 12 or 24 dB/oct. | 20–20000 Hz | 12000 Hz, 12 dB/oct |

Each part has its own checkbox; ticking it turns that part on at its default. The presets each set only their own part and turn Bypass off, so they combine: Rumble Cut, De-mud and Presence together are a common starting point for a voice. A Gain of 0 dB changes nothing. Reset turns the whole EQ off.

The EQ runs after the channel choice and before hum removal and noise reduction. It doesn't change the Loudness read-out, but Normalize in Loudness mode measures through it.

*Agents:* `set_audio` with `eq: {high_pass, low_shelf, band_1, band_2, band_3, high_shelf, low_pass, bypass}`: each part `null` (off) or an object — `high_pass` and `low_pass` `{frequency, slope}`, the shelves `{frequency, gain_db}`, the bands `{frequency, gain_db, q}`. A part you leave out keeps what is stored, `{eq: {high_pass: {}}}` turns one on at its defaults, and `eq: null` turns the EQ off.

## Leveller

The leveller evens out a voice that moves on and off the microphone. It works ahead of time: from the clip's measured loudness and the speech found in it, it works out how loud each phrase is and turns each one up or down toward the target, so a phrase starts at its own level rather than catching up after it begins. In the pauses it holds, so the room isn't lifted while nobody speaks, and just before the next phrase (the last quarter of a second of the pause) it moves to that phrase's level. Its checkbox is labelled **Ride the level**. The section is closed until you open it.

| Control | What it does | Range | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ride the level | Turns the leveller on. | on / off | off |
| Target | Where it aims, in dB from the clip's loudness. Quick picks: −6, −3, 0, 3. | −20 to 10 dB | 0 dB |
| Max Boost | The most it turns the clip up. Quick picks: 6, 12, 18. | 0–24 dB | 12 dB |
| Max Cut | The most it turns the clip down. Quick picks: 3, 6, 12. | 0–24 dB | 12 dB |
| Response | How smoothly it follows the voice within a phrase: changes shorter than this are smoothed out, before and after alike. Quick picks: 1, 2, 4 s. | 0.5–10 s | 2 s |
| Status | Read-only: where it aims, for example "Aims at −41.7 LUFS"; "Waits for its loudness" or "Waits for its speech" until the clip's analysis has run; or "Not levelling:" and why. | — | — |
| Loudness | Read-only: the clip's loudness the target is measured from. | — | — |

It measures each phrase the way a loudness meter does (the BS.1770 weighting), over about three seconds of its speech at a time, never reaching across a pause into the next phrase. A phrase shorter than a second takes the level of the phrase before it (or, at the start, the one after it), so a short "yes" or a cough doesn't get a level of its own. Like the gate's threshold, its target follows the clip's loudness. It needs the clip's speech as well as its loudness: the speech the clip's sound analysis found, or the spans saved for Duck by Speech. Until both are measured it waits, and so does an export. Where speech detection isn't available and the media has no saved speech, it doesn't level the clip and its Status says why. It works the same in the preview and in an export, from wherever playback starts. It runs after the gate, so pauses the gate has turned down stay down, and before the de-esser and the compressor, which then see the levelled voice. With an action selected, Target, Max Boost and Max Cut move smoothly across the action; Response changes at its end.

*Agents:* `set_audio` with `leveller: {target_db, max_up_db, max_down_db, response}` (the response in ticks), `leveller: {}` to turn it on at the defaults, or `leveller: null` to turn it off.

## Compressor

The compressor evens out loud and quiet passages by turning down whatever goes above the threshold. Its checkbox is labelled **Smooth volume levels**.

| Control | What it does | Range | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| Smooth volume levels | Turns the compressor on. | on / off | off |
| Threshold | The level above which the compressor starts turning the sound down. Quick picks: −30, −24, −18, −12, −6. | −60–0 dB | −18 dB |
| Ratio | How strongly it turns down sound above the threshold. 1:1 does nothing; 20:1 acts like a limiter. Quick picks: 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 8, 20. | 1–20 :1 | 3:1 |
| Attack | How quickly it reacts when the level rises. Quick picks: 1, 5, 10, 30, 100 ms. | 0–200 ms | 10 ms |
| Release | How quickly it lets go when the level falls. Quick picks: 50, 100, 200, 500, 1000 ms. | 10–2000 ms | 200 ms |
| Makeup | Gain added afterwards to make up what the compressor took away. Quick picks: 0, 3, 6, 12. | 0–24 dB | 0 dB |

The defaults are a gentle setting for speech. The section summary shows the ratio ("3.0:1"), or "off".

The compressor acts on the clip's sound before the volume, fades and ducking, so moving the Volume slider doesn't change how hard it compresses. If noise reduction is on, it runs before the compressor, which means the threshold is compared against sound that has already been cleaned. Attack and release are measured in milliseconds and aren't rounded to frames.

*Agents:* `set_audio` with `compressor: {threshold_db, ratio, attack, release, makeup_db}` (attack and release in ticks), or `compressor: null` to turn it off.

## Limiter

The limiter holds the clip's peaks under a ceiling, so a quiet recording can be turned up to a delivery loudness without its loudest moments clipping. It is a true-peak limiter: it looks between the samples, where a peak can sit higher than any sample, and it looks a little ahead, so it turns the sound down smoothly just before a peak rather than after it.

| Control | What it does | Range | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| Limit peaks | Turns the limiter on. | on / off | off |
| Ceiling | The highest true peak the clip may reach, after its volume. Quick picks: −1, −2, −3, −6. | −24–0 dBTP | −1 dBTP |
| Release | How quickly the level recovers after a peak. Quick picks: 50, 100, 200, 500. | 1–1000 ms | 100 ms |

The limiter is last in the clip's chain, after the Mix section, and it knows the clip's volume: the ceiling is where the clip's peaks land *after* the Volume slider, so turning the volume up makes the limiter work harder rather than pushing the clip past the ceiling. Fades and ducking only turn a clip down, so they never take it over the ceiling either. Normalize in Loudness mode turns it on for you when a clip needs it.

Each clip's limiter holds that clip alone. Every export also runs a **master limiter** over the whole mix, the same kind of limiter at −1 dBTP (or the export's own ceiling), so two limited clips crossfading, a nest turned up or an action raising a clip's volume still can't take the file past it. The preview has no master limiter. See [Exporting](export.md#loudness).

A track that has a limited clip on it plays every clip on it 3.3 ms later inside Frame Job and places them 3.3 ms earlier to match, so everything still lands on its frame. As with noise reduction, a clip that starts within the first 3.3 ms of the timeline loses that much of its start, and the last 3.3 ms of the track are not heard.

*Agents:* `set_audio` with `limiter: {ceiling_db, release}` (`ceiling_db` from −24 to 0, `release` in ticks from 600 to 600000; `limiter: {}` turns it on at the defaults), or `limiter: null` to turn it off.

import AppKit

/// The EQ section's drawn response (spec §11 AU-36): the EQ and the hum
/// notch together, from `AudioEQDesign.responseDB` — the same design the
/// stage runs — at 48 kHz, where every composition audio track plays
/// (SY-23). 256 log-spaced points from 20 Hz to 20 kHz over ±24 dB, a value
/// past either edge pinned there; a 0 dB line and the decades at 100 Hz,
/// 1 kHz and 10 kHz. Dimmed while bypassed; nothing drawn for a mixed
/// selection (`read` answers nil). Read-only: no handles (out of scope).
final class AudioEQCurveView: NSView, OptionControl {
    static let points = 256
    static let sampleRate = 48_000.0
    static let rangeDB = 24.0
    private static let size = NSSize(width: 252, height: 96)

    /// The 256 frequencies, log-spaced 20 Hz…20 kHz.
    static let frequencies: [Double] = (0..<points).map {
        20 * pow(1000, Double($0) / Double(points - 1))
    }

    private let read: () -> (eq: EqProps?, hum: HumProps?)?
    /// What the drawn response was computed from, and the response.
    private var shown: (eq: EqProps?, hum: HumProps?)?
    private var response: [Double] = []
    private var bypassed = false
    private var computed = false

    init(read: @escaping () -> (eq: EqProps?, hum: HumProps?)?) {
        self.read = read
        super.init(frame: NSRect(origin: .zero, size: Self.size))
        refresh()
    }

    @available(*, unavailable) required init?(coder: NSCoder) {
        fatalError("AudioEQCurveView does not support NSCoder")
    }

    override var isFlipped: Bool { false }
    override var intrinsicContentSize: NSSize { Self.size }

    /// The response in dB at `frequencies` for these props, flat when
    /// nothing is designed. What the view draws; a probe reads it.
    static func response(eq: EqProps?, hum: HumProps?) -> [Double] {
        // Bypass draws the settings dimmed, so the design here ignores it.
        var live = eq
        live?.bypass = false
        guard let settings = AudioEQSettings(eq: live, hum: hum) else {
            return [Double](repeating: 0, count: points)
        }
        return AudioEQDesign.responseDB(settings, frequencies: frequencies, sampleRate: sampleRate)
    }

    func refresh() {
        let now = read()
        let changed = !computed || now?.eq != shown?.eq || now?.hum != shown?.hum
            || (now == nil) != (shown == nil)
        if changed {
            shown = now
            response = now.map { Self.response(eq: $0.eq, hum: $0.hum) } ?? []
            bypassed = now?.eq?.bypass ?? false
            computed = true
            needsDisplay = true
        }
    }

    override func draw(_ dirtyRect: NSRect) {
        let plot = bounds.insetBy(dx: 1, dy: 1)
        NSColor.controlBackgroundColor.setFill()
        plot.fill()
        DS.border.setStroke()
        NSBezierPath(rect: plot.insetBy(dx: -0.5, dy: -0.5)).stroke()
